+# Agent Note: Streamed Tool activity feedback
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-20-streamed-tool-activity-feedback.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The model begins generating a Tool call before the durable `tool/call` event exists. The conversation previously hid `tool-call-delta` blocks, so a slow argument stream or long call showed no Tool row until execution had already completed or produced another formal event. Users could therefore mistake healthy work for a stalled session.
+
+The feedback must span argument generation and execution as one continuous activity, preserve normal Tool identity and ordering, avoid exposing incomplete JSON, and settle without adding persistent completion noise.
+
+## Decision
+
+`ui-conversation` owns a step-local `tool-activity` Conversation Node in addition to the formal `tool-call` lifecycle. The first `tool-call-delta` creates a pending value containing only its stream key, call id when available, name, sequence, and time. Further argument deltas never enter the presentation payload. A finalized Assistant message resolves providers that streamed an empty id, while retry and step closure remove pending rows that never became calls.
+
+The formal Tool Definition reads the preceding activity state when its `tool/call` start is assembled. It inherits the first-delta time as its presentation start, then removes the matching pending value in the same assembly flush. Pending calls are keyed by stream index and kept in insertion order; after the Assistant finalizes, their synthetic placement advances behind that Assistant and then behind each emitted formal call. Formal nodes keep their authoritative `tool/call` sequence, so parallel calls preserve product transcript order while each replaces exactly one pending row. The retained start facts survive step closure so replay and interruption do not reset elapsed time.
+
+`ui-tool` renders a pending value as the same non-expandable title and icon row used by ordinary Tools, with no argument summary or body. One wrapper-owned activity chrome follows both pending and formal rows: a gray elapsed timer and gray `ing` remain visible throughout argument generation and execution, and a Think-style scanning glare crosses the running row. Settlement freezes the timer and stops the glare. The status position is empty by default and reveals gray `Done` only while the finished row is hovered or keyboard-focused. Existing error and interrupted row semantics remain authoritative, and reduced-motion preferences remove the scanning glare.
+
+## Verification
+
+Conversation Definition tests cover immediate visibility from a partial argument delta, empty-id resolution, parallel ordering, same-flush replacement, first-delta timing inheritance, retry cleanup, interruption cleanup, and timing retention after step closure. Tool component tests cover the name-only pending row, running timer updates, terminal timer freezing, the settled `Done` hover contract, wrapper-level scanning, and the reduced-motion exit. The shipped Web replay exercises the assembled browser composition through pending, running, settled, hover, and reload states.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Wait for the formal `tool/call` event.** Rejected because the argument-generation interval remains blank, which is the original stalled-session signal.
+
+**Render partial Tools inside the Assistant reasoning or Markdown row.** Rejected because Tool presentation would cross domain ownership, incomplete arguments could leak into summaries, and the formal call would require a second renderer to remove duplicate rows without flicker.
+
+**Label argument generation as preparing and execution as running.** Rejected because the user-facing fact is continuous activity. A state-name change adds visual churn without helping the user distinguish a healthy call from a stalled session.
+
+**Keep a persistent completed label.** Rejected because historical Tool rows are already self-evidently settled. Hover-only `Done` confirms the state on demand while preserving a quiet transcript.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Tool work becomes visible as soon as the model commits to a streamed Tool block and remains one continuous row through execution. The elapsed timer makes long calls legible, the scanning glare communicates liveness without new copy, and terminal history stays compact.
+
+The pending row deliberately cannot show an argument-derived summary until the formal call exists. A provider that supplies neither a usable streamed identity nor a matching finalized block may fall back to name and oldest-pending correlation; this prevents a ghost row but cannot recover information the event stream never contained.
